    <summary>Shows the sounds your PC is playing as a glow along the screen edges, for deaf and hard-of-hearing people.</summary>
    <description><![CDATA[SoundVisualizer is an open-source accessibility app for deaf and hard-of-hearing people. It listens to the Windows audio output (WASAPI loopback, like desktop audio capture in OBS) and draws a click-through glow along the edges of the screen, on top of videos, music and games.

## Features

- **Direction:** the side a sound comes from glows more. Left and right, plus front and back with 5.1 or 7.1 audio output.
- **Loudness:** louder sounds are drawn bigger.
- **Sound type:** an on-device classifier (YAMNet and Silero VAD through ONNX Runtime) colors sounds as ambient, human sounds, or danger such as sirens and alarms. It can be wrong when many sounds overlap.
- **Display modes:** wave, pad, circle and outline. F2/F3 switch modes and F4 lets you drag the drawing area to fit your screen; hotkeys can be changed.
- **Simple settings:** a settings view where you choose by picture. The launcher works with the keyboard and screen readers such as Narrator and NVDA.
- **Private:** everything runs on your PC. Audio is never saved or sent anywhere, and no account or internet connection is needed.

It was designed and tested with deaf and hard-of-hearing students at Seoul National School for the Deaf.

## Notes

- Windows 10/11, 64-bit only. No .NET installation is needed.
- Games need borderless or windowed mode for the overlay to show on top.
- Settings are saved in `settings.json` next to the program in the package folder. Copy it somewhere safe before upgrading if you want to keep your settings.
- This package downloads the official zip from GitHub Releases and checks its SHA-256 checksum, then adds a Start menu shortcut.
- Also available on the [Microsoft Store](https://apps.microsoft.com/detail/9ngh48vcrfw2).
]]></description>
